hi my name is elizabeth i'm a medical  student  and an artist living in london and i  wanted to share my top tips for success  in medical school interviews for the  last two years i have been helping tens  of students prepare for their interviews  so i've been spending a significant  amount of time  thinking about the interview process  interviews have become something i'm  quite interested in so i thought i'd put  together some of my tips in the hope  that they might help you in your  preparation before i get started i just  wanted to say congratulations  for whatever stage in your application  process you  are in this video we'll touch upon three  main parts the first  is the structure the second is the  content and the third is the delivery  i would ask that you think of the  interviewer as an individual  someone with a limited attention span  who wants to go on their lunch break  who has probably watched five people  interview before you  and therefore you want to be very very  kind  to their patients and very very kind to  their attention  the way that you could do this is by  providing a very  good structure to your answer so the  better your structure  the more empowered that the interviewer  is the more they  know what to expect and what is going on  and what you are going to say next  the more likely they are to listen to  you and therefore  the better for your performance so i  would  make sure that i had a very strong  introduction before i say anything  and this applies to any sort of question  be it a question about you personally  about your suitability into medicine  this could be an ethical  question i would always always start it  by saying the things i'm going to  discuss  are firstly i'm going to touch upon x y  and z  these are things that empower the  listener so they're very very important  to your answer on top of that even as i  go through my answer  i will add small little pointers for  example i will say things like  now that i've finished discussing x i  will jump onto  my second point so you give them prompts  throughout the interview  that keeps your relationship with them  very very close and make sure that  they're not um falling asleep on you  now regarding content the first point is  something that i have definitely made  mistakes on in the past  and this is not answering the question  directly now it sometimes happens that  because the question is a bit unclear to  you  or something that you're perhaps not too  comfortable with or you don't  really know how to approach you might  slightly change it  and this may be intentional or it might  even be completely unintentional  so you change the question itself you  create a new question  which is hopefully quite similar to that  and you answer that  now this is not ideal because you are  not giving them exactly what they were  asking for  so you are highly unlikely to get full  marks on the station or to get full  marks regarding this point my advice  would be  to make sure that before i start  thinking or saying the answer out loud  that i'm actually answering exactly what  they wanted so i would identify  what the main point of their question is  first and make sure that my overall  conclusion  does address that now some questions may  seem  quite vague or you might genuinely not  understand them what i would recommend  in this case  is to ask the interviewer to repeat the  question perhaps or to clarify what they  mean exactly  before you start your answer my second  point regarding content is to make sure  that you are presenting  both sides of an argument this will make  you seem well-rounded  mature intelligent knowledgeable it  brings only  positive things to your answer  especially when it comes to ethical  questions or when it comes to  considerations about your behavior  or someone else's behavior or how you  feel about certain things  i would always always always bring both  sides of the argument into your  answer but there may be cases where you  fully disagree with the other side  you don't like the counter argument i  would say in this case it's more  valuable than ever  to mention it so one way to go about  doing this is to say that  although i strongly feel x i can see how  people  might see the situation in x-way i think  that if you're making a point in any  sort of ethical question  you should actively also try to find a  counter argument  and also present it in order for you to  seem more mature and balanced  my third point is to know the four  pillars of medical ethics  i'm not going to go into this in detail  because it's such a huge topic but  please  google them learn them know them they  are so so  so valuable apart from them being quite  big words they also provide you with  endless free content and things to talk  about when you might be blanking on the  spot under stress i genuinely found  these so useful in my interviews  when i was really stressed and i didn't  know what to think about i would just  think  oh autonomy how does this affect  patients autonomy so these are things i  would definitely  definitely include in a lot of my  answers my last point regarding content  is  perhaps one of the most important and  that is to always  always put the patient first if there is  any sort of question that involves a  patient or a potential patient  you need to consider how your actions or  someone else's actions  may be affecting this patient in  medicine the patient is the center of  our world so it needs to be the center  of your world  too already one key key phrase is  patients best interests and this is not  only regarding their physical health but  it's also regarding their mental health  or  their comfort or how they are feeling or  how they are being treated if there is  any mention of a patient that should  immediately  raise this flag how is this patient  being affected what will be in this  patient's best  interest now i'm moving on to delivery  my first point here is to be  enthusiastic no one should want this  more than you  you definitely want this more than the  interviewer and because they will be  bouncing off your energy most of the  time  it is very very important that you are  quite enthusiastic  and upbeat you need to be engaging and  keen  and it's very very important for your  face and eyes to read that i really  really want to be here i am so so so  keen on being in your university  please please have me this would be the  best thing in the world for me  this is the kind of vibe that you want  to be giving to the interviewer  so one way to achieve this is to smile  with your eyes and also to smile with  your lips and kind of talk through a  smile  of course um don't do this if you're  discussing a very serious situation  because  that that might be problematic  but for most of things it is quite  beneficial to be  very upbeat and happy and my last point  is to be modest rather than boastful  now this is a bit difficult to explain  without sounding too mean  you and both i are quite junior in the  stages of medical training  i would rather come across as someone  who says yeah i am very very keen to  learn i have taken up so many  opportunities but i would never frame  myself as something that i  am not yet because that might come off  as a bit  uninsightful i am aware that this was  quite abstract because i didn't use any  real questions and i do apologize for  that  i might consider making some other  videos where i go into things in a bit  more depth and show you some  good and bad examples of interview  questions so yeah let me know if that's  something that you are interested in  and as always thank you so much for  watching if you made it  to this part and be kind to yourself and  others and don't believe everything you  think  thank you bye
 

